# Test Coverage

Thorough, and concise. The two only pull against each other when tests are
written by enumeration -- a case per input rather than a case per way the code
can be wrong. Cover every way it can be wrong, once each.

## Thorough is about failure modes, not counts

- **The behaviour the change is about gets a test that fails without the
  change.** Write it first and watch it fail. A test that has never been red has
  proven nothing, and the ones that matter most are the ones asserting a bug
  stays fixed.
- **Boundaries, not samples.** Empty, one, many. The first element and the last.
  The value on each side of a threshold. Three inputs from the middle of the
  same range are one test wearing three hats.
- **The error path.** What happens when the call fails, the field is absent, the
  list is empty, the flag is off, the response arrives out of order.
- **The interaction the reader would otherwise take on faith** -- two features
  that have to compose, a rule that has to win over another rule.

Coverage percentage is not the target. A changed line that no test would notice
going wrong is the gap worth closing; a changed line whose only plausible bug is
a typo the compiler catches is not.

## Concise is about lines, not cases

- **One behaviour per test, and a name that states the behaviour** rather than
  the method under test. The name is what a future reader sees in a failure
  report, so it should say what broke.
- **Table-drive the repetition.** When cases differ only in inputs and expected
  output, that is a table, not a screenful of near-identical bodies.
- **Share setup through a helper or a factory with defaults**, and have each
  case override only the field it is about. What a test does not mention should
  be what does not matter to it.
- **Assert the thing, not everything around it.** A whole-object comparison for
  a change to one field buries the subject and breaks on every unrelated edit;
  a snapshot of an entire render asserts nothing anyone can read.
- **Do not test the framework, the language, or a mock you just configured.**
- **Delete rather than accumulate.** A case already covered by a neighbour is
  maintenance with no signal. When a test needs a paragraph of comment to
  explain what it proves, the test is usually the wrong shape.

## Where a test goes

Use the layer the repo already uses for that kind of code -- a pure function
belongs in the unit suite, a component in whatever the project drives real
browsers with. Adding a slow end-to-end case for something a fast one would
have caught is the expensive way to be thorough, and the one that gets the suite
ignored.
